 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery.** The server maintains WebSocket sessions and delivers committed event revisions promptly to connected browsers. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because its intentional delay conflicts with fast board updates; a hosted realtime service was rejected because it couples the board to a provider. This choice requires replayable revisions, reliable publication, reconnect handling, and capacity planning for 800 simultaneous browsers. See [the delivery decision](decisions/committed-change-delivery.md) for the selection criteria and alternatives.
